.TH SPRING_GRID 1 2026-10-29 "spring_grid" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
spring_grid \- real-time 2-D spring-mass lattice with spectral diagnostics
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B spring_grid
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B spring_grid
simulates an N\(muM grid of point masses joined by cardinal and
(optionally) diagonal bonds, and draws it in an SDL2 window together with a
spectral panel.
Each bond family can be a Hookean spring or a rigid rod.
.PP
The lattice can be perturbed with the mouse and keyboard, switched between
four boundary conditions, prestressed, given mass defects or disorder, and
surrounded by an absorbing layer.
The spectral panel shows mode occupation, the space\-time dispersion
S(q,\ \(*w) with the analytic branches overlaid, mode energies over time,
and kinetic, potential and total energy.
.PP
All quantities are in lattice units: the rest spacing is
.IR a\ =\ 1 ,
time is in seconds, masses in kg, spring constants in N per lattice unit.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Lattice
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-cols " \fIN\fR"
Number of columns, 4\(en1024.
Default 64.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-rows " \fIN\fR"
Number of rows, 4\(en1024.
Default 64.
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-mass " \fIM\fR"
Node mass.
Default 1.0.
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-k " \fIK\fR"
Cardinal spring constant.
Default 400.
.TP
.BI \-\-kdiag " K"
Diagonal spring constant.
Default: equal to
.IR k .
The long-wave elastic response is isotropic only when
.IR k\ =\ 2k_d .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-diag
Start with the diagonal bonds off.
.TP
.B \-\-rod\-cardinal
Make the cardinal bonds rigid rods (holonomic constraints enforced with
RATTLE).
.TP
.B \-\-rod\-diagonal
Make the diagonal bonds rigid rods.
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-bc " \fINAME\fR"
Boundary condition:
.BR free ,
.BR pinned ,
.BR clamped
or
.BR periodic .
Default
.BR free .
Periodic requires even
.I cols
and
.IR rows .
.TP
.BI \-\-prestrain " E"
Spring rest length
.IR L0\ =\ (1\-E)|r| ,
0\ \(<=\ E\ <\ 0.5.
Takes effect only under clamped and periodic boundaries; rods keep their
reference length.
Default 0.
.SS Dynamics
.TP
.BR \-g ", " \-\-damping " \fIG\fR"
Uniform linear drag \(*g in kg/s.
Default 0.4.
.TP
.BI \-\-dt " DT"
Integration step in seconds.
Default 5e\-4.
The program warns if \(*w_max\(*Dt exceeds 0.5.
With rods, which do not limit the step, 2e\-3 is usually fine.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-speed " \fIS\fR"
Simulated seconds per wall-clock second, up to 64.
Default 1.
.SS "Disorder and boundaries"
.TP
.BI \-\-disorder " NAME"
Mass distribution:
.BR none ,
.B light
(a light defect at the centre),
.B heavy
(a heavy defect at the centre) or
.B random
(every mass random).
Default
.BR none .
.TP
.BI \-\-defect\-light " F"
Mass ratio of the light defect.
Default 0.2.
.TP
.BI \-\-defect\-heavy " F"
Mass ratio of the heavy defect.
Default 5.
.TP
.BI \-\-disorder\-width " W"
Random masses are
.IR m(1+W\(*xU[\-1,1]) ,
0\ \(<=\ W\ <\ 1.
Default 0.5.
.TP
.B \-\-absorb
Start with the absorbing layer on.
.TP
.BI \-\-absorb\-width " N"
Absorbing layer thickness in nodes.
Default 10.
.TP
.BI \-\-absorb\-gamma " G"
Peak extra drag in the layer; the profile is quadratic in depth.
Default
.RI \(sr( km ).
.SS Analysis
.TP
.BI \-\-fput\-strain " S"
Peak strain of the mode set up by the
.B F
and
.B Shift\-F
keys.
Default 0.15.
.TP
.BI \-\-nt " N"
Number of time samples in the \(*w\-q window; a power of two, at least 16.
Default 256.
.TP
.BI \-\-seed " N"
Seed for the random number generator.
Default: the current time.
.SS Window
.TP
.BI \-\-width " W"
.TQ
.BI \-\-height " H"
Initial window size in points.
Default: 1200\(mu800, shrunk to fit the display.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.SH KEYS AND MOUSE
.TP 12
.B Left\-drag
Grab a mass and pull it; release to throw.
The mass follows the pointer smoothly; the release velocity is a
least-squares fit over the last 80\ ms of pointer motion.
.TP
.B Right\-click
Random velocity kick, with net momentum removed (free and periodic) and net
angular momentum removed (free).
.TP
.B Space
Pluck the centre mass.
.TP
.B G
Large random kick.
.TP
.B R
Reset to the rest lattice.
.TP
.B B
Cycle boundary condition: free, pinned, clamped, periodic.
.TP
.B D
Diagonal bonds on/off.
.TP
.B C
Cardinal bonds: springs \(<> rigid rods.
.TP
.B X
Diagonal bonds: springs \(<> rigid rods.
.TP
.B T
Cycle prestrain: 0, 2, 5, 10%.
.TP
.B M
Cycle masses: uniform, light defect, heavy defect, random.
.TP
.B A
Absorbing layer on/off.
.TP
.BR + " / " \-
Multiply / divide the damping by 1.25; below 0.001 it becomes 0.
.TP
.B 0
Damping off, or restore the previous value.
.TP
.B F
FPUT preset: periodic boundary, diagonal springs, no tension, damping,
absorber or rods; excite the lowest longitudinal mode at
.B \-\-fput\-strain
and run at 4\(mu speed.
.TP
.B Shift\-F
Excite the lowest mode of the selected branch with the current settings.
.TP
.BR Tab " / " Shift\-Tab
Next / previous spectral view.
.TP
.B V
Branch shown: L, T, L+T.
.TP
.B P
Pause.
.TP
.BR , " / " .
Halve / double the simulation speed.
.TP
.B H
Hide or show the help text.
.TP
.BR Q ", " Esc
Quit.
.SH SPECTRAL VIEWS
.TP
.B "1. Occupation"
.RI | v_b(q) |\(S2,
the spatial transform of the velocity field projected on branch
.I b
and exponentially averaged.
It shows which modes carry energy, not their frequencies.
.TP
.B "2. Dispersion"
.IR S_b(q_x,\ \(*w) :
a Hann-windowed time Fourier transform of the last
.I nt
samples on the lowest-q_y line, normalised per q column, with the analytic
\(*w_L(q) and \(*w_T(q) dashed.
Read it after the "filling" counter reaches 100%.
.TP
.B "3. Mode energies"
.IR E\ =\ (m/2)(P\(S2\ +\ \(*w\(S2Q\(S2)
for the lowest eight x\-wavenumbers on the lowest-q_y line, against time.
.TP
.B "4. Energy"
Kinetic, potential and total energy against time.
The state panel also shows the relative drift dE/E while the system is
conservative and undisturbed.
.PP
The spatial transform matches the boundary condition: DCT\-II for free and
pinned, DST\-I on the interior for clamped, real-to-complex FFT for
periodic.
At each q the two components are projected onto the eigenvectors of the
analytic dynamical matrix, labelled quasi-longitudinal (L) and
quasi-transverse (T).
.SH PHYSICS
The integrator is velocity Verlet with exact drag applied in two half
steps:
.IR v\ \(<-\ v\ exp(\-\(*g\(*Dt/2m) .
With \(*g\ =\ 0 it is symplectic and the energy error stays bounded.
.PP
Rigid rods enforce
.IR |r+u_b\-u_a|\ =\ |r|
with RATTLE.
The rods of one family form straight chains; each chain's linearised
constraints are tridiagonal and solved exactly, sweeping over chain families
until every length is within about 5e\-9\ a.
Rods store no energy.
Each rod is coloured by its constraint force: red for tension, blue for
compression.
Under clamped or periodic boundaries cardinal rods lock the lattice
completely; moves they forbid are refused and the state panel says so.
.PP
The README describes the dispersion relation, the boundary conditions and
the FPUT preset in full.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SG_SCRIPT
A semicolon-separated list of
.IB frame : action\fR[\fB:\fIarg\fR]
items run at the given frame numbers, for headless screenshots and smoke
tests.
The actions are:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.BI key: name\fR[\fB+shift\fR]
(an SDL key name);
.IP \(bu 2
.BI mdown: x,y\fR,
.BI mmove: x,y
and
.BI mup: x,y
(the left mouse button, in window points);
.IP \(bu 2
.BI shot: file.bmp\fR;
.IP \(bu 2
.BR quit .
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on normal exit or after
.BR \-\-help ;
1 if SDL or memory initialisation fails;
2 for an invalid command line.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B spring_grid \-c 32 \-r 32 \-\-bc periodic
A smaller torus.
.TP
.B spring_grid \-\-bc clamped \-\-no\-diag \-\-prestrain 0.05
A drum: tension gives the cardinal lattice transverse stiffness.
.TP
.B spring_grid \-\-disorder light \-\-absorb
After a pluck (Space), the absorber removes everything except the energy
trapped in the defect's local mode.
.TP
.B spring_grid \-\-kdiag 200
The isotropic case: circular wavefronts, c_L/c_T\ =\ \(sr3.
.TP
.B spring_grid \-\-rod\-cardinal \-\-dt 2e\-3
Rigid rows and columns coupled by diagonal springs, at about real time.
.TP
.B SG_SCRIPT="5:key:F;900:shot:fput.bmp;901:quit" xvfb\-run spring_grid
Run the FPUT preset headlessly and save a screenshot.
